Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

ZERO WINDOW MANAGER

The window manager is original Zero code inside the monolithic kernel.
It provides independent terminal sessions and two keyboard-driven layouts.
Two terminals open at startup; window1 has focus. All windows remain open
when they leave the visible area. No image assets or external GUI library
are required.

Keyboard
F1  create a terminal and focus it
F2  close the focused terminal; the last terminal stays open
F3  focus the next open terminal
F4  focus the previous open terminal
F5  switch between tiled and horizontally scrolling layouts
Held function keys do not repeatedly create or close windows.

Commands, also usable over serial
windows             list live IDs, selected volumes, focus and capacity
window new          create and focus a terminal
window close        close the focused terminal
window next         focus the next live ID, wrapping at the end
window prev         focus the preceding live ID
window 3            focus the live terminal with ID3
window tile         select the tiled layout
window scroll       select the horizontal strip
window toggle       toggle layouts

IDs belong to slots, not positions on screen. Closing a window does not
renumber other windows; a later creation reuses the lowest free ID. Closing
the focused window selects the next higher live ID, wrapping if needed.
No disk file is deleted when closing a window.

Display
The preferred native mode is1920x1080. At this resolution a960x540 logical
workspace is drawn with2x scaling: terminal cells are16x32 physical pixels,
and frames, borders, margins and hints scale together. Other firmware modes
keep1x scaling. Firmware without Full HD can use the smaller validated modes.
The pixel command always uses physical framebuffer coordinates.

Layouts
Tile: a single window fills the workspace. Two share it side by side.
Groups of three use a master window on the left and two stacked on the
right. Focusing a window in another group automatically shows that group;
hidden groups retain all session state. Minimum supported display640x400.
Scroll: each terminal is a column in one ordered horizontal workspace.
Focus centers its column, with clipped neighbors at the edges. Next/previous
focus moves the viewport; keyboard navigation is immediate, not animated.
The current renderer displays at most three columns at once.

Each window owns its incomplete command, overflow flag, selected volume and
the most recent7168 bytes of terminal output. The byte ring is reflowed for
its current text width when the layout changes. clear resets only that
window's output. Its input limit remains480 bytes. There is no command-line
recall, manual scrollback navigation, mouse interaction or clipboard yet.
New terminals inherit the selected volume of the window that created them.
Switching focus preserves a partially typed command. Device modifier state
and serial CRLF framing are global, so a trailing LF never submits a command
in a different window after a focus change.

Memory and scheduling
Each slot occupies8192 bytes in the unused middle of the loader-owned pool.
Capacity is computed from that pool:88 windows with the current1MiB UEFI
pool,344 with the current3MiB BIOS pool. This is not unlimited allocation or
all installed RAM. Insufficient room refuses creation without losing other
windows. The main and child native stacks retain their reserved regions.

Terminal sessions share the existing command thread and filesystem engine.
Commands complete serially; these windows are not isolated user processes.
Focus changes occur only while the engine is waiting for input, never during
a filesystem operation. Filesystem caches remain shared and valid across
windows. Closing a terminal discards its volatile input/output history.

The graphics thread owns the framebuffer while the manager is active. It
repaints dirty visible windows, checks layout generations after yields and
clips drawing to the screen/window. A fixed ASCII grid bounds rendering
scratch space. animate toggles the focused caret blink; theme changes colors.
pause1 freezes display updates, but input/commands still run and output is
retained; resume1 shows the latest state. Computation remains independently
pausable. A missing GUI falls back to the focused VGA text terminal or serial.

Sources
wm/init.zero.part      pool validation and initial sessions
wm/switch.zero.part    deferred safe creation/close/focus/layout operations
wm/commands.zero.part  command validation and action requests
wm/list.zero.part      bounded streaming inventory
wm/worker.zero.part    original compositor, ring replay and glyph renderer
wm/integrate.sh        checked source integration; --check does not edit
wm/SESSION.ztext and wm/RENDERER.ztext document the internal ABI.
wm/design/ contains the explicitly labelled earlier static design previews.

The same sources compile for BIOS/UEFI and x86-32/x86-64. See verification.ztext
for completed tests, hashes and the boundary of native VirtualBox testing.
